  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{-2}{y}^{3}=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}-{e}^{x}y={x}^{2}{y}^{5}$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x=-x{y}^{3}$",
  "worked_solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-{e}^{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{2},\\,n=5.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
